Practical 2: Installation and Configuration of Virtualization using KVM on Ubuntu Linux (Including System Not Supported Case)
bjective
•	Check virtualization support 
•	Install KVM 
•	Configure virtualization 
•	Create virtual machines 
•	Handle unsupported systems 
________________________________________
Requirements
•	Ubuntu Linux 
•	Internet Connection 
•	Minimum 4 GB RAM 
•	CPU virtualization support (Intel VT-x / AMD-V) 
________________________________________
Theory
KVM (Kernel-based Virtual Machine) is an open-source virtualization technology built into Linux kernel.
KVM allows:
•	Multiple virtual machines 
•	Different operating systems 
•	Isolation between systems 
•	Efficient resource utilization 
Main Components:
•	KVM 
•	QEMU 
•	libvirt 
•	virt-manager 
________________________________________
STEP 1: Open Terminal
Shortcut:
Ctrl + Alt + T
________________________________________
STEP 2: Check Virtualization Support
Run:
egrep -c '(vmx|svm)' /proc/cpuinfo
________________________________________
Output Cases
Case 1: Supported
If output is:
1
or greater than 1:
✅ Virtualization supported.
Proceed to installation.
________________________________________
Case 2: Not Supported
If output is:
0
Then virtualization is disabled or unsupported.
________________________________________
WHAT TO DO IF SYSTEM IS NOT SUPPORTED
________________________________________
Method 1: Enable Virtualization from BIOS
Restart system.
Press:
•	F2 
•	F10 
•	DEL 
•	ESC 
(depending on laptop/PC)
Open:
BIOS/UEFI Settings
Find:
•	Intel VT-x 
•	AMD-V 
•	Virtualization Technology 
Enable it.
Save and Exit.
________________________________________
STEP 3: Check Again
Run:
egrep -c '(vmx|svm)' /proc/cpuinfo
If output becomes:
1
System supports virtualization now.
________________________________________
Method 2: If Hardware Does NOT Support Virtualization
If CPU does not support VT-x/AMD-V:
KVM cannot run.
Use alternative virtualization software:
Software	Type
Oracle VM VirtualBox	Desktop Virtualization
VMware Workstation	Virtualization
QEMU only	Emulator
________________________________________
STEP 4: Update System
Run:
sudo apt update
Then:
sudo apt upgrade -y
________________________________________
STEP 5: Install KVM Packages
Run:
sudo apt install qemu-kvm libvirt-daemon-system libvirt-clients bridge-utils virt-manager -y
________________________________________
STEP 6: Verify KVM Installation
Run:
sudo systemctl status libvirtd
________________________________________
Output
active (running)
________________________________________
STEP 7: Check KVM Modules
Run:
lsmod | grep kvm
________________________________________
Output Example
Intel CPU:
kvm_intel
kvm
AMD CPU:
kvm_amd
kvm
________________________________________
STEP 8: Add User to Groups
Run:
sudo usermod -aG libvirt $USER
Then:
sudo usermod -aG kvm $USER
________________________________________
STEP 9: Reboot System
Run:
sudo reboot
________________________________________
STEP 10: Verify KVM
After reboot:
virsh list --all
________________________________________
Output
 Id   Name   State
--------------------
________________________________________
STEP 11: Open Virtual Machine Manager
Run:
virt-manager
________________________________________
STEP 12: Create First VM
1.	Click: 
Create New Virtual Machine
2.	Select: 
Local install media
3.	Select Ubuntu ISO file. 
4.	Allocate: 
•	RAM 
•	CPU 
•	Disk Space 
5.	VM Name: 
UbuntuVM1
6.	Click: 
Finish
________________________________________
STEP 13: Create Second VM
Repeat process.
VM Name:
UbuntuVM2
________________________________________
STEP 14: Run Different Files on Both VMs
________________________________________
VM1
Create Python file:
nano file1.py
Code:
print("Running on VM1")
Run:
python3 file1.py
________________________________________
VM2
Create second file:
nano file2.py
Code:
print("Running on VM2")
Run:
python3 file2.py
________________________________________
Output
VM1:
Running on VM1
VM2:
Running on VM2
________________________________________
Important Commands
Command	Purpose
virt-manager	Open GUI
virsh list --all	List VMs
python3 file.py	Run Python file
`lsmod	grep kvm`
sudo systemctl status libvirtd	Check KVM status
